    "result_text": "The full suite is green (the project's only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, which now passes). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented soft-match \"possible duplicate\" detection for `POST /api/owners`. When a new owner isn't a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's `lastName` and `postcode` while giving a different telephone, it's still created and flagged.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`FlagOwnerPossibleDuplicate.java`** (new pipeline function) \u2014 the single piece of new logic. Operating on the built `Owner` before save, it scans stored owners for one with the same `lastName` (case-insensitive) and `postcode` but a different telephone, and sets `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf` accordingly (lowest matching id for determinism). It mirrors the existing `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount`/`FlagOwnerBulkSignup` step style.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step in between `assignNamesakeCount` and `save`, under the same transaction governance, so it runs after the hard-duplicate check (`RequireUniqueIdentity`) and before persistence.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added `possibleDuplicate` (Boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (Integer) persistent fields with accessors.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the two read-only properties to the `Owner` response schema (regenerates `OwnerDto`; MapStruct maps them by name automatically).\n\n5. **DB schemas** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added `possible_duplicate` and `possible_duplicate_of` columns to the `owners` table.\n\nThe detection stays a small, single-responsibility function reusing the existing repository and step-wiring conventions rather than bloating an existing class.",
